;;; DRAFT -- break!
;;; Edited 2026-08-03 by Priya.
;;; AUTHORED, not model-seeded. The drafting batch
;;; (scratch/pairs/draft_records.py, see draft_run.log) skipped
;;; this verb because an edited draft already existed, so no raw
;;; model output for it survives. Claiming model provenance here
;;; would be provenance nobody can check.
;;; Structure, examples, row programs, caveats and provenance are
;;; authored, not generated.
;;;
;;; Every :note is the literal placeholder PENDING-ENGINE. NOTHING in this
;;; file was run. The notes are filled by a deterministic script once the
;;; cons-cell engine lands. A printed value here would be a value nobody ran.
;;;
;;; Spec basis: ../spec-pack/break-bang.slatl -- read ../spec-pack/00-SOURCES.md first,
;;; the normative SRFI-1 text is NOT in this repo.

(verb
  :name "break!"
  :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "state-change"
  :signature "(break! pred lst) -> two values: (prefix rest)"
  :summary "Everything before the first element that satisfies pred, and the rest, as two values cut from lst's own cells."
  :explanation "break! stops at the first element that MATCHES, where span! stops at the first that fails. That single inversion is the whole difference: (break! pred lst) and (span! (lambda (x) (not (pred x))) lst) are the same call. It is worth having both names because the sentence a caller wants to write is sometimes \"up to the first delimiter\" and sometimes \"while it is still a digit\", and forcing one of those through a negation is how off-by-one predicates get written. The suite exercises it at list.sld:545-554 and requires both returned lists to be built from the argument's cells. The bang is a licence to destroy. After the call the cells that made up the argument have been re-used, so the variable you passed in no longer names what it named. Bind the result and read only that."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"
     :code "(define xs (list 3 1 4 1 5 9))\n(let-values (((before rest) (break! even? xs)))\n  (display (list 'before before 'rest rest))\n  (newline))"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate"
     :code ";; the matching element heads the SECOND value, it is not consumed.\n(define xs (list 'a 'b 'comma 'c))\n(let-values (((field rest) (break! (lambda (t) (eq? t 'comma)) xs)))\n  (display (list 'field field 'rest-head (car rest)))\n  (newline))"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"
     :code ";; break! with a never-true predicate: whole list in front, empty rest,\n;; and the argument has been cut at its very end.\n(define xs (list 1 3 5))\n(let-values (((before rest) (break! even? xs)))\n  (display (list 'before before 'rest-empty (null? rest)))\n  (newline))"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
  )
  :caveats ("The argument is destroyed when the prefix is non-empty. list.sld:545-554 asserts both results are built from the argument's cells."
             "The element that satisfies pred is NOT consumed; it is the head of the second value. Callers splitting on a delimiter must drop it themselves."
             "When the first element already satisfies pred the prefix is empty, nothing is written, and the second value is the argument."
             "It returns TWO values; bind them with let-values.")
  :drawbacks ("Having both break! and span! doubles the surface for one behaviour, and a reader must check which polarity is in play."
             "The delimiter surviving into the remainder is a standing source of one-element-too-many bugs in split loops."
             "As with span!, the destruction is data-dependent and so the hazard is intermittent.")
  :usecases ("Splitting a self-owned token list at the first delimiter."
             "Reading up to a sentinel in a buffer you built, keeping the sentinel for the next stage to inspect."
             "Error scanning: everything before the first bad record, plus the bad record and what follows.")
  :related ("break"
             "span!"
             "take-while!"
             "drop-while"
             "split-at!")
  :learn (:concept "Polarity: stopping at a match versus stopping at a failure"
          :prerequisites ("break" "span!" "let-values" "not")
          :progression "Write one split loop with span! and the same loop with break! and notice which one needed a negation. That is how you learn which to reach for.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 linear-update procedures; conformance evidence scratch/r7rs-conformance/larceny/list.sld:545-554; signature derived from the break record in scheme/reference/39-list.slat. Drafted 2026-08-03; NOT RUN."
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define xs (list 3 1 4 1 5))\n(let-values (((b r) (break! even? xs)))\n  (display (list b r))\n  (newline))"
     :math-program "(define a (list 3 1 4 1))\n(define b (list 3 1 4 1))\n(let-values (((ba ra) (break even? a)) ((bb rb) (break! even? b)))\n  (display (list (equal? ba bb) (equal? ra rb)))\n  (newline))"
     :cs-program "(define xs (list 'a 'stop 'b))\n(let-values (((before rest) (break! (lambda (t) (eq? t 'stop)) xs)))\n  (display (list 'delimiter-kept (car rest)))\n  (newline))"
     :problem "The operator wants everything up to the first delimiter in a token list they own. Candidates: break (both halves fresh), span with a negated predicate (same result, one negation to get wrong), or break! (no allocation, source destroyed). The reason to prefer break! over span!-with-not here has nothing to do with speed: the sentence is \"until the delimiter\", and writing it as \"while not the delimiter\" is where the polarity bug enters."
     :explanation "eng: the field comes back and the delimiter is still at the head of the remainder. math: break! agrees with break on both values, and with span! under negation. cs: both values are made of the argument's cells, so the original is gone even though nothing was allocated."
     :meta "Choosing the verb whose polarity matches your sentence is a correctness measure, not a style preference.")
    (:eng "Match the verb to the sentence. Negations added at the call site are defects waiting for a rush."
     :math "break and span are De Morgan duals; the implementation can be one function, the API should not be."
     :cs "The delimiter staying in the remainder is a deliberate design choice and the single most-missed detail of this verb.")
  )
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Cutting a sequence just before the first element that matches, keeping both sides."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(let-values (((b r) (break! even? (list 3 1 4 5))))\n  (display (list b r))\n  (newline))")
             (:domain "a CSV field ending at the first comma" :code "(define row (list #\\a #\\b #\\, #\\c))\n(let-values (((field rest) (break! (lambda (c) (char=? c #\\,)) row)))\n  (display (list 'field field 'rest rest))\n  (newline))")
             (:domain "a log read up to the first error line" :code "(define lines (list 'ok 'ok 'error 'ok))\n(let-values (((clean from-error) (break! (lambda (l) (eq? l 'error)) lines)))\n  (display (list 'clean clean 'from-error from-error))\n  (newline))")
     )
     :explanation "In list, the cut lands just before the first match. A CSV field ends at the first comma and the comma stays for the parser to step over. A log read up to the first error keeps the error line at the head of what remains, because the error is the thing the next stage cares about. All three share a match that is a boundary marker rather than a member of what precedes it."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever the matching element is significant to the NEXT stage rather than to the one that stopped.")
    (:eng "Leave the marker where the next reader will look for it."
     :math "The prefix is characterised by containing no match; the remainder by starting with one."
     :cs "Delimiter handling is where hand-written parsers most often lose or duplicate an element.")
  )
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define xs (list 3 1 4))\n(let-values (((b r) (break! even? xs)))\n  (display (list 'before b 'rest r))\n  (newline))"
     :math-program "(define (break-laws? p xs)\n  (let-values (((b r) (break p xs)))\n    (and (not (any p b))\n         (or (null? r) (p (car r)))\n         (equal? (append b r) xs))))\n(display (list (break-laws? even? (list 3 1 4 5)) (break-laws? even? (list 2 3)) (break-laws? even? (list 1 3))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define a (list 3 1 4))\n(define b (list 3 1 4))\n(let-values (((ba ra) (break! even? a)) ((bb rb) (span! odd? b)))\n  (display (list 'dual (equal? ba bb) (equal? ra rb)))\n  (newline))"
     :claim "break! returns the maximal prefix of lst containing no element satisfying pred, together with the remainder, which is empty or begins with an element satisfying pred. Their append is the original contents, both are built from lst's cells, and the pair equals what span! returns under a negated predicate."
     :invariant "forall pred, proper lst. (b, r) = break!(pred, lst) satisfies not any(pred, b) and (r = () or pred(car(r))) and equal?(append(b, r), lst-as-it-was) and break!(pred, lst) = span!(not-pred, lst)"
     :meta "The pattern is a De Morgan dual pair. What would falsify the claim: a match inside the prefix, a remainder not headed by a match, or a break!/span! disagreement under negation -- the last being the one an implementation sharing a single helper is most likely to get wrong in exactly one direction.")
    (:eng "Test the dual explicitly. A shared implementation can satisfy one polarity and invert the other."
     :math "not-any over the prefix and pred over the head of the rest are the two halves of maximality here."
     :cs "The never-matches case leaves the whole list in front and an empty remainder; the always-matches case is its mirror.")
  )
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (first-field row sep)\n  (let-values (((f rest) (break (lambda (c) (char=? c sep)) row)))\n    (list 'field f 'rest rest)))\n(display (first-field (list #\\a #\\, #\\b) #\\,))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (split-on p xs)\n  (if (null? xs)\n      (list (list))\n      (let-values (((b r) (break p xs)))\n        (if (null? r)\n            (list b)\n            (cons b (split-on p (cdr r)))))))\n(display (split-on even? (list 1 3 4 5 6 7)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (until-error lines)\n  (let-values (((clean rest) (break (lambda (l) (eq? l 'error)) lines)))\n    (list 'clean-count (length clean) 'stopped (not (null? rest)))))\n(display (until-error (list 'ok 'ok 'error 'ok)))\n(newline)"
     :base "break! cuts a list just before the first element satisfying a predicate and returns both parts."
     :emergent "eng: field extraction, the parser's smallest step. math: repeated breaking with an explicit cdr past the delimiter is full string-splitting, and that cdr is the line the delimiter-keeping rule exists to make you write on purpose. cs: an early-stop scan that reports both the clean run and whether it stopped, which is more useful than either alone."
     :meta "The family is delimiter-driven parsing. What emerges is that the awkward-looking choice to keep the delimiter is what lets the caller decide whether to drop it, and that decision differs between splitting and error reporting.")
    (:eng "An API that keeps the marker lets the caller choose; one that eats it has already chosen for them."
     :math "Splitting on a delimiter is repeated breaking plus an explicit skip; the skip is a separate decision."
     :cs "Reporting whether the scan stopped early is why break returns two values instead of a truncated list.")
  )
)
